what is a claim Trump is the greatest US  president in history Berlin is the  capital of Germany the government should  provide universal health care I'm  wearing white shoes these are all claims  a claim is a statement and claims assert  something something that could be true  or it could be false and often a claim  is a statement that needs to be proven  or supported and this is done through  the use of an argument which also  involves premises evidence and reasoning  but before even trying to determine  whether a claim is true or false it's  important to know what kind of claim  you're dealing with because claims can  fall under two categories descriptive  claims and normative claims a  descriptive claim says something about  what is or what could be descriptive  claims are objective impartial and don't  involve value judgments such and such is  the case for instance Fox News has a  website the trueness or falseness of  this descriptive claim can be determined  without invoking values go to Fox  news.com and there's the website on the  other hand a normative claim makes an  evaluation relative to something else  which means making a value judgment for  instance Fox News is awful this is a  normative claim because it involves a  value this claim relates to standards of  awfulness the claim can't stand on its  own without consideration for what it  means for something to be awful you can  think of descriptive claims as asserting  what is and normative claims as  asserting what ought to be let's look at  some other descriptive claims Tom only  eats ice cream Michel often doesn't stop  at stop signs Sarah is wearing a green  shirt these claims alone don't tell you  anything about my perspective on these  matters whether or not I feel that these  things are good or bad or how I think  these things might affect something else  but here are some normative claims a  about the same people Tom should rethink  his dietary habits Michael is a bad  driver Sarah should dress differently  before using a green screen these claims  use implicit inferences about how people  ought to behave in reference to other  values good driving good nutrition and  proper green-screen attire let's take my  claims about Sarah my descriptive claim  is Sarah is wearing a green shirt this  is objective and doesn't involve other  values but how do I know Sarah is  wearing a green shirt because I can see  her shirt and it's green but take my  normative claim Sarah should dress  differently before using a green screen  I'm making an assertion about what ought  to be  I'm implicitly referencing other values  that aren't present in my claim the  value of not blending in with the  background and the value of that being  preferable for reasons that are based on  other values like making a good video  for example understanding all of this  helps you understand how certain claims  might need to interact with other claims  not all claims can stand on their own  without being inextricably linked to  other ideas values and arguments that  need to come into play wrapping your  head around normative versus descriptive  claims can help you recognize bias in  yourself and in other people it can also  allow you to avoid trying to solve a  problem merely by describing its parts  or on the other hand prescribing  solutions to problems without properly  describing what the problem is and this  is a fundamental concept and beginning  to understand how different types of  claims need different types of  justifications this miniseries is made  possible in part by brilliant if you  want to become an even better critical  thinker you should check out this online  learning service brilliant is a site  where you can learn about logic  fallacies multi-level thinking and  countless other topics related to  philosophy mathematics and science  what sets brilliant apart is that you're  actually learning by working hands-on  with  life problems one of their principles is  effective learning is active and this  makes a lot of sense there's a ton you  can learn from passive activities  reading books watching online videos but  you're never really taking your  understanding to the next level and  applying what you're learning until  you're able to work with real-world  scenarios and that's what brilliant  allows you to do master anything from  probability to physics to the concepts  of artificial intelligence and  everything in between  sign up for free by going to brilliant  org slash critical thinking I've put a  link in the description under this video  and the first 200 people who visit that  URL will get 20% off an annual  membership visit brilliant org slash  critical thinking
 

